Title: BricsCAD refuses to LOAD a file containing a form it executes happily
Status: open (not blocking; the probe harness works around it)
Found: 2026-08-31, chasing probes-suite-crashes-bricscad

THE SHAPE OF IT

probes/sources/probe-foreach-scope.lsp contained, inside one function:

    (list (foreach e (list 1 2 3) (* e 10))
          (foreach e nil 99))

and earlier, in the same function, `(foreach e (list 1 2 3))' with no
body. BricsCAD would not LOAD the file -- on macOS and on Windows,
before a single probe ran, with no diagnostic reaching the results
file: the process simply sat until the harness's 180-second timeout.

All three of those forms EXECUTE CORRECTLY in BricsCAD. The suite now
holds them as strings and READs them when the case runs, and BricsCAD
answers every one -- including returning (1 2 3) for the body-less
FOREACH, which is a divergence worth its own ticket
(foreach-empty-body-return-diverges.issue).

So the rejection is in whatever reads a file being LOADED, and not in
the evaluator. A form BricsCAD will run is a form BricsCAD will not
read from a file.

WHICH FORM, EXACTLY, IS STILL UNKNOWN

The per-form split (PROBE_SPLIT_SUITES) narrowed it to ONE FUNCTION --
form 10 of 13, CAD-PROBE-FOREACH--RETURN-VALUE -- which is what finally
ended the guessing. It did not narrow it further, because all three
candidates sat in that one function:

  - FOREACH in EXPRESSION POSITION, as an argument to LIST
  - FOREACH over an EMPTY LIST
  - FOREACH with NO BODY
  - or only some combination of them

THE EXPERIMENT IS NOW WRITTEN -- probes/sources/probe-load-refusal.lsp,
plus probe:load-refusal:bricscad:{macos,windows}. It holds the three
candidates as three separate top-level forms, written OUT rather than
hidden in strings, because loading them is the question. Run alone and
SPLIT, each becomes its own file with its own marker, so the last
marker names the form BricsCAD would not read.

Three things had to be true for that to be safe, and now are:

  - QUARANTINE. The suite is marked `quarantine' in the manifest, so a
    default `make probe' SKIPS it and only PROBE_SUITES=load-refusal
    runs it. Without that, adding this file would have turned every
    ordinary BricsCAD probe run into a hang -- the trap this whole
    ticket is about, re-laid by the ticket's own fix.
  - PROBE_TIMEOUT. The harness's 180 s is fine for a suite that works;
    an experiment that EXPECTS a hang runs at 45 s, because the Windows
    runner is serial and nothing else should queue behind a deliberate
    hang.
  - allow_failure. A failed job here is a RESULT, not a regression.

Verified on the clautolisp baseline both ways: a default run produces
265 results and ZERO from this suite, and the named run loads all four
forms with their markers and answers all three cases `ok'.

ROUND 1 -- 2026-09-02, BricsCAD on macOS. THE CULPRIT IS NAMED.

    ((KIND . "step") (AT . "loaded probe-load-refusal.lsp form 1 ...")
                     (VALUE . "CAD-PROBE-LR--EXPRESSION-POSITION"))

and nothing after it. Form 1 LOADED -- its marker carries the function
it defined. Form 2's marker never appears.

  (list (foreach e (list 1 2 3) (* e 10)))   LOADED   -- innocent
  (foreach e nil 99)                         REFUSED  -- the culprit
  (foreach e (list 1 2 3))                   not reached

BRICSCAD WILL NOT READ A FOREACH WHOSE LIST ARGUMENT IS A LITERAL NIL,
from a file it is loading. It executes the same form perfectly well
when the form is READ at run time -- that is how the empty-list return
value was measured, and it is nil there, as in AutoCAD.

FOREACH IN EXPRESSION POSITION IS INNOCENT, which is worth stating
because it was the candidate I would have bet on: a loop used as an
argument looks like the unusual thing, and it is not.

ONE RUN FINDS AT MOST ONE CULPRIT, and it finds the FIRST -- a refusal
hangs the engine, so everything after it is never attempted. The
suite's form ORDER is therefore part of the experiment, not
housekeeping: the body-less form, still unknown, now goes FIRST and the
known culprit goes LAST. That is written at the head of the suite so
the next person does not reorder it tidily and lose the design.

WHAT THIS MEANS IN PRACTICE, and it is not nothing: `(foreach x nil
...)' is what a program contains when the list argument is a constant
that happens to be empty -- a degenerate case a generator or a macro
expansion produces easily. Such a file loads in AutoCAD and in
clautolisp and DOES NOT LOAD in BricsCAD, with no diagnostic: the
process sits there. Anyone shipping AutoLISP to both hosts should know
it, which is why this ticket stays open until the third candidate is
settled and the whole thing can be written up as a portability note.

STILL MISSING: whether the body-less FOREACH is also refused. Round 2
answers that, since it is now first.

WHY IT IS NOT URGENT

Nothing in clautolisp depends on the answer. It matters for two
practical reasons and neither is pressing: AutoLISP written for
clautolisp or AutoCAD may fail to LOAD in BricsCAD for reasons no
diagnostic explains, and anyone writing a probe suite will meet this
again. The second is already handled -- the rule is written at the head
of probe-foreach-scope.lsp and the harness has the split.

THE LESSON, which is the durable part

A load that HANGS writes nothing about itself. The marker that would
name the file comes after the load that never returns, and
VL-CATCH-ALL-APPLY cannot catch a process that is not coming back. The
file therefore says only that the PREVIOUS load finished -- a silence.
It was read as evidence twice, and both readings were wrong: first
blaming a suite that had loaded fine, then a form that turned out
innocent. PROBE_SPLIT_SUITES exists so the last marker NAMES the form.
